-- Passage wg12-informative-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e7e3a0fd-c9e7-5cfa-ab5e-8d45f6968b80', 'wg12-informative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Losing the Night Sky$q$, $q$(1) On a clear night far from any town, an observer can see roughly 2,500 stars with the naked eye. (2) In the center of a large city, that number may drop to fewer than fifty. (3) The difference is caused by light pollution, the excess artificial light that brightens the night sky and washes out faint stars.

(4) Light pollution takes several forms. (5) Skyglow is the diffuse brightening over populated areas, visible as a dome of light from many kilometers away. (6) Glare is excessive brightness that causes visual discomfort, and light trespass occurs when light spills into places where it is not wanted, such as a bedroom window. (7) Skyglow is the form that most affects astronomers.

(8) Light pollution also affects wildlife. (9) In a five-year study along the coast of Delmora County, biologist Tomas Achterberg found that sea turtle hatchlings on brightly lit beaches often crawled toward streetlights instead of toward the ocean. (10) Many nocturnal insects, which navigate partly by moonlight, circle artificial lights until they are exhausted.

(11) Some towns have responded by changing their lighting. (12) The town of Pell Harbor replaced 1,800 streetlights with shielded fixtures that point light downward. (13) After the change, a sky brightness meter in the town park recorded a 30 percent reduction in skyglow, and the town's lighting bill dropped as well.

(14) Light pollution is bad.$q$, 'draft')
