-- Passage wg12-informative-writing-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cee10173-2559-5bbe-bef4-df294d6d2c8c', 'wg12-informative-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Cables on the Ocean Floor$q$, $q$(1) When a student in one country joins a video call with a classmate overseas, the data rarely travels by satellite. (2) More than 95 percent of international internet traffic instead moves through submarine cables, bundles of glass fiber laid across the ocean floor. (3) Each fiber, thinner than a human hair, carries information as pulses of laser light.

(4) A typical deep-sea cable is surprisingly slender, about the width of a garden hose. (5) Near the shore, where anchors and fishing gear pose risks, cables are wrapped in layers of steel wire and buried in trenches beneath the seabed. (6) In deep water, where few hazards exist, they simply rest on the bottom.

(7) Light weakens as it travels through glass, so the signal cannot cross an ocean unaided. (8) Every 60 to 80 kilometers, a device called a repeater amplifies the light pulses before they fade too much to read. (9) Repeaters require electricity, which reaches them through a copper layer inside the cable from power stations on each coast.

(10) Cables do break, most often when ship anchors drag across them. (11) When this happens, a specialized repair ship locates the damaged section, raises the cable to the surface with a grappling hook, splices in a new segment and lowers it back down. (12) In 2022, the repair ship Kestrel Dawn fixed a break off the coast of the Marisol Islands in eleven days. (13) Traffic was moved to other cables.$q$, 'draft')
