insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9c94246b-d7fc-59a8-9300-55ce6b37545e', 'wg12-paragraph-transitions-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Strategy on Two Wheels$q$, $q$(1) Wheelchair basketball looks familiar to anyone who knows the standing game, since the court, the hoop, and the ball are all the same size. (2) The rules, however, have been adapted in ways that reward a distinct set of skills. (3) A player may push the wheels twice before dribbling, passing, or shooting, and a third push without dribbling counts as traveling. (4) ____, success depends as much on controlling the chair as on handling the ball.

(5) Each player is assigned a classification number based on functional ability, ranging from low numbers for athletes with the most limited trunk movement to higher numbers for those with greater mobility. (6) This limit forces coaches to balance their lineups rather than simply sending out the five players with the greatest mobility. (7) The five players on the court together may not exceed a set total of points. (8) As a result, athletes with very different abilities all have meaningful roles on the team.

(9) Watching a skilled team in action reveals how much strategy the sport involves. (10) Players with high classification numbers often lean far out of their chairs to grab rebounds, while teammates set picks by positioning their chairs to block defenders. (11) Players also learn to tip their chairs onto one wheel to gain extra reach on a shot. (12) ____, the athletes who excel are those who treat the chair not as an obstacle but as part of their game. (13) Many players also compete in track events during the off season.$q$, 'draft')
